 ## Overview
-The Möller-Trumbore algorithm computes the intersection of a ray (origin $O$, direction $D$) with a triangle defined by vertices $V_0, V_1, V_2$, returning the intersection distance $t$ and barycentric coordinates $(u, v)$ [S1, unofficial]. It solves for the intersection point by translating the ray origin and changing the vector basis, yielding a $(t, u, v)^T$ vector without explicitly computing or storing the triangle's plane equation [S4, S5, unofficial]. This approach offers minimal memory overhead and is considered the fastest routine for triangles lacking precomputed plane equations [S5, unofficial].
